WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.828
Error handling is one of the most common
places for leaks to appear.

2
00:00:03.908 --> 00:00:06.998
The normal path may allocate, use, and
free memory correctly,

3
00:00:07.078 --> 00:00:09.574
while failure paths return early, skip a
release,

4
00:00:09.654 --> 00:00:11.615
or lose track of what has been acquired.

5
00:00:11.695 --> 00:00:15.914
Defensive C review should inspect the
failure paths with the same seriousness as the

6
00:00:15.994 --> 00:00:16.886
successful path.

7
00:00:16.966 --> 00:00:21.185
Functions that allocate several resources
need cleanup logic for partial success.

8
00:00:21.265 --> 00:00:25.365
A parser might allocate a header object,
then a list, then several nested strings.

9
00:00:25.445 --> 00:00:29.546
A setup function might acquire memory, a
file handle, and a configuration buffer.

10
00:00:29.626 --> 00:00:30.814
If the third step fails,

11
00:00:30.894 --> 00:00:35.173
cleanup should release the first two
without assuming the entire object is complete.

12
00:00:35.253 --> 00:00:39.472
Releasing resources in many different
branches increases the chance of missing one

13
00:00:39.552 --> 00:00:39.849
path.

14
00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:40.939
As a function grows,

15
00:00:41.019 --> 00:00:44.407
separate returns and scattered free calls
can become hard to audit.

16
00:00:44.487 --> 00:00:48.706
A predictable cleanup section can make
ownership state easier to review because all

17
00:00:48.786 --> 00:00:51.341
releases happen in a known order from a
known place.

18
00:00:51.421 --> 00:00:54.927
A single cleanup section is not a magic
rule, but it is a useful pattern.

19
00:00:55.007 --> 00:00:57.800
It can release resources in reverse
acquisition order,

20
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.099
check whether a pointer is non-null before
freeing, and return a consistent status.

21
00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:06.458
This approach works best when pointers are
initialized to known values and ownership

22
00:01:06.538 --> 00:01:08.143
state is updated consistently.

23
00:01:08.223 --> 00:01:12.323
Cleanup code can be safe to run for
resources that were never initialized when it is

24
00:01:12.403 --> 00:01:13.354
designed that way.

25
00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:15.811
For example, freeing a null pointer is
safe in C,

26
00:01:15.891 --> 00:01:19.813
but calling a custom cleanup routine on an
uninitialized structure may not be.

27
00:01:19.893 --> 00:01:22.924
Each cleanup helper should document what
states it accepts.

28
00:01:23.004 --> 00:01:26.331
Failure behavior should leave the program
in a known, valid state.

29
00:01:26.411 --> 00:01:27.422
If a function fails,

30
00:01:27.502 --> 00:01:30.711
callers should know whether ownership
remains with the caller,

31
00:01:30.791 --> 00:01:32.752
whether partial output was cleaned up,

32
00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:35.862
and whether any returned object must be
ignored or released.

33
00:01:35.942 --> 00:01:40.340
A predictable failure contract prevents
leaks from spreading across call boundaries.
